  "dom_id": "step-q215469",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Solve the absolute value inequality $|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2}\\le 1.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we isolate the absolute value on the left side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{2x+1}{4}|-\\frac{3}{2} & \\le 1 \\\\ |\\frac{2x+1}{4}| & \\le \\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we convert the absolute value inequality into a compound inequality to remove the absolute value. \n\nThe inequality specifies every number $\\frac{2x+1}{4}$ whose distance from zero is less than or equal to $\\frac{5}{2}.$ This corresponds to the following compound inequality:\n\n$$\n\\text{ }-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4}\\le \\frac{5}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve the compound inequality using the usual methods.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2}\\le \\frac{2x+1}{4} & \\le \\frac{5}{2} \\\\ -10\\le 2x+1 & \\le 10 \\\\ -11\\le 2x & \\le 9 \\\\ -\\frac{11}{2}\\le x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
